WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:08.296 --> 00:11.556 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference September 21,

00:11.886 --> 00:17.326 align:middle
2022 CHAIR POWELL.

00:17.726 --> 00:18.286 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:19.696 --> 00:21.816 align:middle
My colleagues and I
are strongly committed

00:21.816 --> 00:24.476 align:middle
to bringing inflation back
down to our 2 percent goal.

00:25.466 --> 00:27.566 align:middle
We have both the tools
we need and the resolve

00:27.566 --> 00:30.276 align:middle
that it will take to restore
price stability on behalf

00:30.276 --> 00:31.956 align:middle
of American families
and businesses.

00:33.276 --> 00:34.906 align:middle
Price stability is
the responsibility

00:34.906 --> 00:36.606 align:middle
of the Federal Reserve
and serves

00:36.656 --> 00:38.126 align:middle
as the bedrock of our economy.

00:38.966 --> 00:40.046 align:middle
Without price stability,

00:40.046 --> 00:42.096 align:middle
the economy does
not work for anyone.

00:42.766 --> 00:44.386 align:middle
In particular, without
price stability,

00:44.386 --> 00:46.596 align:middle
we will not achieve
a sustained period

00:46.596 --> 00:49.116 align:middle
of strong labor market
conditions that benefit all.

00:50.916 --> 00:54.026 align:middle
Today the FOMC raised
its policy interest rate

00:54.026 --> 00:57.336 align:middle
by   percentage
point, and we anticipate

00:57.336 --> 00:59.896 align:middle
that ongoing increases
will be appropriate.

01:01.196 --> 01:03.966 align:middle
We are moving our policy
stance purposefully to a level

01:03.966 --> 01:05.506 align:middle
that will be sufficiently
restrictive

01:06.026 --> 01:07.776 align:middle
to return inflation
to 2 percent.

01:08.396 --> 01:11.546 align:middle
In addition, we are
continuing the process

01:11.546 --> 01:14.176 align:middle
of significantly reducing the
size of our balance sheet.

01:14.796 --> 01:18.486 align:middle
I will have more to say about
today's monetary policy actions

01:18.486 --> 01:21.016 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

01:22.586 --> 01:25.166 align:middle
The U.S. economy has slowed

01:25.166 --> 01:28.346 align:middle
from the historically
high growth rates of 2021,

01:28.746 --> 01:29.906 align:middle
which reflected the reopening

01:29.906 --> 01:32.236 align:middle
of the economy following
the pandemic recession.

01:33.676 --> 01:35.696 align:middle
Recent indicators
point to modest growth

01:35.696 --> 01:36.776 align:middle
of spending and production.

01:38.206 --> 01:40.036 align:middle
Growth in consumer
spending has slowed

01:40.036 --> 01:41.636 align:middle
from last year's rapid pace,

01:42.096 --> 01:44.656 align:middle
in part reflecting lower
real disposable income

01:44.656 --> 01:46.196 align:middle
and tighter financial
conditions.

01:47.516 --> 01:51.036 align:middle
Activity in the housing sector
has weakened significantly,

01:51.246 --> 01:53.466 align:middle
in large part reflecting
higher mortgage rates.

01:54.916 --> 01:57.696 align:middle
Higher interest rates and
slower output growth also appear

01:57.696 --> 01:59.646 align:middle
to be weighing on
business fixed investment,

02:00.376 --> 02:03.676 align:middle
while weaker economic growth
abroad is restraining exports.

02:05.276 --> 02:07.866 align:middle
As shown in our Summary
of Economic Projections,

02:08.016 --> 02:10.856 align:middle
since June, FOMC
participants have marked

02:10.856 --> 02:12.936 align:middle
down their projections
for economic activity,

02:13.446 --> 02:16.126 align:middle
with the median projection
for real GDP growth standing

02:16.126 --> 02:20.536 align:middle
at just 0.2 percent this year
and 1.2 percent next year,

02:21.086 --> 02:22.516 align:middle
well below the median estimate

02:22.916 --> 02:24.716 align:middle
of the longer-run
normal growth rate.

02:26.986 --> 02:28.246 align:middle
Despite the slowdown in growth,

02:28.246 --> 02:30.246 align:middle
the labor market has
remained extremely tight,

02:30.466 --> 02:32.556 align:middle
with the unemployment
rate near a 50-year low,

02:33.096 --> 02:36.736 align:middle
job vacancies near historical
highs, and wage growth elevated.

02:37.906 --> 02:41.936 align:middle
Job gains have been robust, with
employment rising by an average

02:41.936 --> 02:45.566 align:middle
of 378,000 jobs per month
over the last three months.

02:46.916 --> 02:49.146 align:middle
The labor market continues
to be out of balance,

02:49.706 --> 02:52.286 align:middle
with demand for workers
substantially exceeding the

02:52.286 --> 02:53.926 align:middle
supply of available workers.

02:55.206 --> 02:58.246 align:middle
The labor force participation
rate showed a welcome uptick

02:58.246 --> 03:01.386 align:middle
in August but is little changed
since the beginning of the year.

03:02.786 --> 03:06.106 align:middle
FOMC participants expect
supply and demand conditions

03:06.106 --> 03:08.516 align:middle
in the labor market to come
into better balance over time,

03:09.246 --> 03:11.596 align:middle
easing the upward pressure
on wages and prices.

03:12.996 --> 03:16.496 align:middle
The median projection in the SEP
for the unemployment rate rises

03:16.496 --> 03:18.636 align:middle
to 4.4 percent at
the end of next year,

03:19.006 --> 03:21.726 align:middle
½ percentage point higher
than in the June projections.

03:22.766 --> 03:26.126 align:middle
Over the next three years, the
median unemployment rate runs

03:26.126 --> 03:30.006 align:middle
above the median estimate of
its longer-run normal level.

03:31.316 --> 03:34.556 align:middle
Inflation remains well above
our 2 percent longer-run goal.

03:34.946 --> 03:36.866 align:middle
Over the 12 months
ending in July,

03:36.996 --> 03:39.816 align:middle
total PCE prices
rose 6.3 percent;

03:40.636 --> 03:42.826 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

03:42.826 --> 03:45.446 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
4.6 percent.

03:46.846 --> 03:48.566 align:middle
In August, the 12-month change

03:48.676 --> 03:52.536 align:middle
in the consumer price index
was 8.3 percent, and the change

03:52.536 --> 03:55.496 align:middle
in the core CPI was 6.3 percent.

03:56.886 --> 03:59.466 align:middle
Price pressures remain
evident across a broad range

03:59.466 --> 04:00.546 align:middle
of goods and services.

04:01.916 --> 04:04.746 align:middle
Although gasoline prices have
turned down in recent months,

04:04.836 --> 04:07.246 align:middle
they remain well above
year-earlier levels,

04:07.726 --> 04:10.116 align:middle
in part reflecting Russia's
war against Ukraine,

04:10.686 --> 04:12.946 align:middle
which has boosted prices
for energy and food

04:12.946 --> 04:15.696 align:middle
and has created additional
upward pressure on inflation.

04:16.696 --> 04:19.406 align:middle
The median projection in the SEP

04:19.546 --> 04:24.116 align:middle
for total PCE inflation is 5.4
percent this year and falls

04:24.116 --> 04:28.506 align:middle
to 2.8 percent next year,
2.3 percent in 2024,

04:28.936 --> 04:30.976 align:middle
and 2 percent in 2025.

04:32.026 --> 04:35.436 align:middle
Participants continue to
see risks to inflation

04:35.436 --> 04:37.326 align:middle
as weighted to the upside.

04:38.496 --> 04:39.936 align:middle
Despite elevated inflation,

04:40.036 --> 04:42.276 align:middle
longer-term inflation
expectations appear

04:42.276 --> 04:45.516 align:middle
to remain well anchored, as
reflected in a broad range

04:45.516 --> 04:48.656 align:middle
of surveys of households,
businesses, and forecasters

04:49.256 --> 04:51.096 align:middle
as well as measures
from financial markets.

04:51.976 --> 04:54.396 align:middle
But that is not grounds
for complacency;

04:54.756 --> 04:58.526 align:middle
the longer the current bout
of high inflation continues,

04:58.596 --> 05:00.356 align:middle
the greater the chance
that expectations

05:00.356 --> 05:02.356 align:middle
of higher inflation
will become entrenched.

05:04.306 --> 05:06.306 align:middle
The Fed's monetary
policy actions are guided

05:06.306 --> 05:08.646 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

05:08.906 --> 05:10.826 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people.

05:12.146 --> 05:14.326 align:middle
My colleagues and
I are acutely aware

05:14.326 --> 05:16.886 align:middle
that high inflation imposes
significant hardship,

05:16.956 --> 05:20.756 align:middle
as it erodes purchasing power,
especially for those least able

05:20.756 --> 05:23.396 align:middle
to meet the higher costs
of essentials like food,

05:23.466 --> 05:24.736 align:middle
housing, and transportation.

05:25.216 --> 05:29.066 align:middle
We are highly attentive to the
risks that high inflation poses

05:29.066 --> 05:32.316 align:middle
to both sides of our mandate,
and we are strongly committed

05:32.316 --> 05:34.606 align:middle
to returning inflation to
our 2 percent objective.

05:35.256 --> 05:37.366 align:middle
At today's meeting,

05:37.366 --> 05:39.746 align:middle
the Committee raised the target
range for the federal funds rate

05:39.816 --> 05:43.036 align:middle
by   percentage point,
bringing the target range

05:43.036 --> 05:44.736 align:middle
to 3 to 3¼ percent.

05:45.746 --> 05:47.136 align:middle
And we are continuing
the process

05:47.136 --> 05:49.516 align:middle
of significantly reducing the
size of our balance sheet,

05:49.716 --> 05:50.936 align:middle
which plays an important role

05:51.306 --> 05:53.376 align:middle
in firming the stance
of monetary policy.

05:55.016 --> 05:57.876 align:middle
Over coming months, we will be
looking for compelling evidence

05:58.066 --> 06:00.046 align:middle
that inflation is
moving down consistent

06:00.046 --> 06:02.306 align:middle
with inflation returning
to 2 percent.

06:03.496 --> 06:07.426 align:middle
We anticipate that ongoing
increases in the target range

06:07.426 --> 06:09.116 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate will be appropriate;

06:09.776 --> 06:12.136 align:middle
the pace of those increases
will continue to depend

06:12.136 --> 06:15.006 align:middle
on the incoming data
and the evolving outlook

06:15.006 --> 06:15.706 align:middle
for the economy.

06:16.966 --> 06:19.266 align:middle
With today's action, we
have raised interest rates

06:19.406 --> 06:21.026 align:middle
by 3 percentage points
this year.

06:21.776 --> 06:23.316 align:middle
At some point, as the stance

06:23.316 --> 06:25.046 align:middle
of monetary policy
tightens further,

06:25.776 --> 06:27.666 align:middle
it will become appropriate
to slow the pace

06:27.666 --> 06:31.076 align:middle
of increases while we assess
how our cumulative policy

06:31.076 --> 06:33.436 align:middle
adjustments are affecting
the economy and inflation.

06:34.786 --> 06:37.266 align:middle
We will continue to make our
decisions meeting by meeting

06:37.606 --> 06:40.156 align:middle
and communicate our thinking
as clearly as possible.

06:42.206 --> 06:44.826 align:middle
Restoring price stability will
likely require maintaining a

06:44.826 --> 06:47.026 align:middle
restrictive policy
stance for some time.

06:47.926 --> 06:50.726 align:middle
The historical record
cautions strongly

06:50.726 --> 06:52.816 align:middle
against prematurely
loosening policy.

06:54.276 --> 06:56.656 align:middle
As shown in the SEP,
the median projection

06:56.656 --> 06:57.686 align:middle
for the appropriate level

06:57.686 --> 07:01.646 align:middle
of the federal funds rate is 4.4
percent at the end of this year,

07:02.196 --> 07:04.416 align:middle
1 percentage point higher
than projected in June.

07:05.956 --> 07:09.006 align:middle
The median projection rises
to 4.6 percent at the end

07:09.006 --> 07:13.276 align:middle
of next year and declines to
2.9 percent by the end of 2025,

07:13.716 --> 07:16.486 align:middle
still above the median estimate
of its longer-run value.

07:17.656 --> 07:19.986 align:middle
Of course, these projections
do not represent a Committee

07:19.986 --> 07:22.726 align:middle
decision or plan, and no
one knows with any certainty

07:22.726 --> 07:25.096 align:middle
where the economy will be
a year or more from now.

07:27.206 --> 07:30.186 align:middle
We are taking forceful and
rapid steps to moderate demand

07:30.186 --> 07:32.446 align:middle
so that it comes into better
alignment with supply.

07:33.606 --> 07:35.896 align:middle
Our overarching focus
is using our tools

07:35.896 --> 07:38.416 align:middle
to bring inflation back
down to our 2 percent goal

07:38.946 --> 07:41.306 align:middle
and to keep longer-term
inflation expectations

07:41.366 --> 07:41.986 align:middle
well anchored.

07:43.416 --> 07:46.606 align:middle
Reducing inflation is likely
to require a sustained period

07:46.606 --> 07:50.106 align:middle
of below-trend growth, and
there will very likely be some

07:50.106 --> 07:52.046 align:middle
softening of labor
market conditions.

07:53.046 --> 07:56.626 align:middle
Restoring price stability is
essential to set the stage

07:56.986 --> 07:58.476 align:middle
for achieving maximum employment

07:58.476 --> 08:00.866 align:middle
and stable prices
over the longer run.

08:01.946 --> 08:04.616 align:middle
We will keep at it until we
are confident the job is done.

08:06.646 --> 08:09.606 align:middle
To conclude: We understand that
our actions affect communities,

08:09.676 --> 08:11.436 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

08:12.086 --> 08:14.436 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

08:15.296 --> 08:16.866 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

08:16.986 --> 08:18.726 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

08:18.816 --> 08:20.106 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

08:20.616 --> 08:22.736 align:middle
Thank you, and I look
forward to your questions.

08:23.266 --> 08:23.976 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

08:24.176 --> 08:24.876 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

08:25.766 --> 08:30.906 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

08:30.986 --> 08:32.566 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek from
the New York Times.

08:32.566 --> 08:34.496 align:middle
I wonder if you could
give us a little detail

08:34.496 --> 08:38.006 align:middle
around how you'll know when to
slow down these rate increases

08:38.006 --> 08:40.516 align:middle
and how you'll eventually
know when to stop.

08:42.346 --> 08:42.906 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:43.176 --> 08:46.386 align:middle
So I will answer-I will
answer your question directly,

08:46.386 --> 08:48.556 align:middle
but I want to start
here today by saying

08:48.556 --> 08:50.256 align:middle
that my main message
has not changed

08:50.256 --> 08:52.866 align:middle
at all since Jackson Hole.

08:52.956 --> 08:55.946 align:middle
The FOMC is strongly
resolved to bring inflation

08:55.946 --> 08:58.446 align:middle
down to 2 percent,
and we will keep at it

08:58.446 --> 08:59.536 align:middle
until the job is done.

09:00.846 --> 09:07.606 align:middle
So the way we're thinking about
this is, the overarching focus

09:07.606 --> 09:09.106 align:middle
of the Committee is
getting inflation back

09:09.106 --> 09:09.986 align:middle
down to 2 percent.

09:10.566 --> 09:13.496 align:middle
To accomplish that, we think
we'll need to do two things,

09:13.496 --> 09:16.926 align:middle
in particular: to achieve a
period of growth below trend;

09:16.926 --> 09:19.056 align:middle
and also some softening
in labor market conditions

09:19.056 --> 09:21.066 align:middle
to foster a better
balance between demand

09:21.066 --> 09:22.136 align:middle
and supply in the labor market.

09:23.036 --> 09:26.766 align:middle
So on the first, the
Committee's forecasts and those

09:26.766 --> 09:30.306 align:middle
of most outside forecasters do
show growth running below its

09:30.306 --> 09:32.556 align:middle
longer-run potential
this year and next year.

09:33.116 --> 09:36.536 align:middle
On the second, though, so far
there's only modest evidence

09:36.586 --> 09:38.566 align:middle
that the labor market
is cooling off.

09:39.686 --> 09:42.256 align:middle
Job openings are down
a bit, as you know;

09:42.436 --> 09:45.146 align:middle
quits are off their all-time
highs; there's some signs

09:45.196 --> 09:47.306 align:middle
that some wage measures
may be flattening out

09:47.376 --> 09:50.146 align:middle
but not moving up; payroll gains
have moderated but not much.

09:50.986 --> 09:54.506 align:middle
And in light of the high
inflation we're seeing,

09:54.586 --> 09:57.476 align:middle
we think we'll need to-and
in light of what I just said,

09:57.476 --> 10:01.006 align:middle
we think that we'll need
to bring our funds rate

10:01.426 --> 10:04.816 align:middle
to a restrictive level and to
keep it there for some time.

10:05.076 --> 10:08.496 align:middle
So, what will we be looking
at, I guess, is your question.

10:08.616 --> 10:10.806 align:middle
So we'll be looking
at a few things.

10:10.806 --> 10:13.496 align:middle
First, we'll want to see growth
continuing to run below trend,

10:13.756 --> 10:15.196 align:middle
we'll want to see movements

10:15.196 --> 10:17.626 align:middle
in the labor market showing
a return to a better balance

10:17.626 --> 10:20.246 align:middle
between supply and
demand, and, ultimately,

10:20.246 --> 10:21.666 align:middle
we'll want to see clear evidence

10:21.666 --> 10:25.086 align:middle
that inflation is moving
back down to 2 percent.

10:26.596 --> 10:29.636 align:middle
So that's what we'll
be looking for.

10:29.676 --> 10:34.026 align:middle
In terms of reducing
rates, I think we'd want

10:34.026 --> 10:36.376 align:middle
to be very confident that
inflation is moving back

10:36.376 --> 10:41.176 align:middle
down to 2 percent before
we would consider that.

10:41.766 --> 10:42.606 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

10:43.496 --> 10:46.936 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

10:46.936 --> 10:47.956 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

10:47.956 --> 10:48.906 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

10:49.346 --> 10:53.596 align:middle
Can you talk about how you
factor in the variable lags

10:53.596 --> 10:59.126 align:middle
on inflation and the
extent to which the outlook

10:59.126 --> 11:01.906 align:middle
for rates should be seen
as linear in the sense

11:01.906 --> 11:03.116 align:middle
that you keep raising rates?

11:03.616 --> 11:06.376 align:middle
Well, can you envision a time
when there's a pause to kind

11:06.836 --> 11:09.016 align:middle
of look at what has been
wrought in the economy

11:09.706 --> 11:10.796 align:middle
from the rate increases?

11:10.796 --> 11:11.106 align:middle
Thank you.

11:11.666 --> 11:12.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:12.676 --> 11:18.766 align:middle
Sure. So, of course, monetary
policy does, famously,

11:18.766 --> 11:20.166 align:middle
work with long and
variable lags.

11:20.896 --> 11:22.726 align:middle
The way I think of it is,

11:22.726 --> 11:24.906 align:middle
our policy decisions
affect financial

11:24.906 --> 11:26.236 align:middle
conditions immediately.

11:26.236 --> 11:28.676 align:middle
In fact, financial conditions
have usually been affected well

11:28.676 --> 11:30.386 align:middle
before we actually
announce our decisions.

11:30.996 --> 11:33.006 align:middle
Then, changes in
financial conditions begin

11:33.006 --> 11:37.026 align:middle
to affect economic
activity fairly quickly,

11:37.026 --> 11:37.846 align:middle
within a few months.

11:38.336 --> 11:43.076 align:middle
But it's likely to take some
time to see the full effects

11:43.156 --> 11:45.726 align:middle
of changing financial
conditions on inflation.

11:45.896 --> 11:48.436 align:middle
So we are very much
mindful for that.

11:48.976 --> 11:51.666 align:middle
And that's why I noted in
my opening remarks that,

11:51.926 --> 11:54.446 align:middle
at some point, as the stance
of policy tightens further,

11:54.776 --> 11:56.556 align:middle
it will become appropriate
to slow the pace

11:56.556 --> 11:59.286 align:middle
of rate hikes while we assess
how our cumulative policy

11:59.286 --> 12:01.446 align:middle
adjustments are affecting
the economy and inflation.

12:02.296 --> 12:03.486 align:middle
So that's how we
think about that.

12:03.796 --> 12:05.296 align:middle
Your second question-sorry-was?

12:05.616 --> 12:06.056 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

12:06.056 --> 12:07.916 align:middle
Is there a point in time
you could see pausing?

12:07.916 --> 12:08.586 align:middle
Is it linear?

12:08.586 --> 12:10.606 align:middle
Do you keep raising rates,
or is there-oh, sorry,

12:11.936 --> 12:14.346 align:middle
I should know better than to
not talk with a microphone.

12:14.346 --> 12:14.586 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:15.176 --> 12:16.996 align:middle
I should know better than to
answer your second question.

12:17.236 --> 12:18.326 align:middle
[Laughter] STEVE LIESMAN.

12:18.656 --> 12:19.956 align:middle
Well, there you go.

12:20.666 --> 12:21.326 align:middle
Is it linear?

12:21.326 --> 12:23.286 align:middle
Do you keep raising
rates, or is there a pause

12:23.286 --> 12:25.356 align:middle
that you could envision
where you kind of figure

12:25.796 --> 12:30.216 align:middle
out what has happened to the
economy and give time to catch

12:30.216 --> 12:32.486 align:middle
up in the real economy-the
rate increase time to catch

12:32.486 --> 12:33.186 align:middle
up in the real economy?

12:33.186 --> 12:33.516 align:middle
Thank you.

12:33.686 --> 12:34.066 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:34.066 --> 12:36.836 align:middle
So I think it's very hard to say

12:36.836 --> 12:40.416 align:middle
with precise certainty the
way this is going to unfold.

12:40.686 --> 12:44.396 align:middle
As I mentioned, what we think
we need to do and should do is

12:44.396 --> 12:45.546 align:middle
to move our policy rate

12:45.546 --> 12:47.676 align:middle
to a restrictive level
that's restrictive enough

12:48.166 --> 12:50.436 align:middle
to bring inflation
down to 2 percent,

12:50.436 --> 12:51.646 align:middle
where we have confidence
of that.

12:51.816 --> 12:55.086 align:middle
And what you see in the SEP
numbers is people's views

12:55.086 --> 12:59.756 align:middle
as of today, as of this meeting,
as to the kind of levels

12:59.756 --> 13:00.736 align:middle
that will be appropriate.

13:00.736 --> 13:03.696 align:middle
Now, those will evolve
over time,

13:04.326 --> 13:07.966 align:middle
and I think we'll just
have to see how that goes.

13:08.326 --> 13:10.786 align:middle
There is a possibility,
certainly, that we would go

13:10.816 --> 13:13.716 align:middle
to a certain level
that we're confident in

13:13.766 --> 13:16.146 align:middle
and stay there for a time.

13:16.316 --> 13:17.706 align:middle
But we're not at that level.

13:17.766 --> 13:22.356 align:middle
Clearly, today we're, we've
just moved, I think, probably,

13:22.356 --> 13:26.696 align:middle
into the very lowest level of
what might be restrictive, and,

13:26.696 --> 13:28.046 align:middle
certainly, in my
view and the view

13:28.046 --> 13:30.386 align:middle
of the Committee,
there's a ways to go.

13:32.016 --> 13:32.476 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

13:32.596 --> 13:33.176 align:middle
Okay. Rachel.

13:33.336 --> 13:35.936 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

13:36.466 --> 13:37.676 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

13:37.796 --> 13:39.406 align:middle
Rachel Siegel from
the Washington Post.

13:39.406 --> 13:40.856 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

13:41.446 --> 13:43.976 align:middle
The projections show the
unemployment rate rising

13:43.976 --> 13:49.106 align:middle
to 4.4 percent next year, and,
historically, that kind of rise

13:49.106 --> 13:51.196 align:middle
in the unemployment rate would
typically bring a recession

13:51.196 --> 13:51.616 align:middle
with it.

13:52.036 --> 13:54.746 align:middle
Should we interpret that
to mean no soft landing,

13:55.096 --> 13:57.406 align:middle
and is that kind of rise
necessary to get inflation down?

13:58.116 --> 13:59.066 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:59.356 --> 14:01.876 align:middle
Right. So you're
right-in the SEP,

14:02.026 --> 14:03.576 align:middle
there's what I would
characterize

14:03.576 --> 14:06.086 align:middle
as a relatively modest increase
in the unemployment rate

14:06.086 --> 14:07.366 align:middle
from a historical perspective,

14:07.366 --> 14:09.836 align:middle
given the expected
decline in inflation.

14:09.836 --> 14:10.856 align:middle
Now, why is that?

14:11.416 --> 14:15.656 align:middle
So, really, that is
what we generally expect

14:16.386 --> 14:20.306 align:middle
because we see the current
situation as outside

14:20.306 --> 14:23.086 align:middle
of historical experience
in a number of ways,

14:23.086 --> 14:24.426 align:middle
and I'll mention
a couple of those.

14:24.776 --> 14:26.376 align:middle
First-and you know
these-but first,

14:26.376 --> 14:29.686 align:middle
job openings are incredibly
high relative to the number

14:29.686 --> 14:31.646 align:middle
of people looking for work.

14:31.646 --> 14:34.746 align:middle
It's plausible, I'll say,
that job openings could come

14:34.746 --> 14:37.906 align:middle
down significantly-and they
need to-without as much

14:37.906 --> 14:40.716 align:middle
of an increase in
unemployment as has happened

14:40.716 --> 14:42.306 align:middle
in earlier historical episodes.

14:42.576 --> 14:43.426 align:middle
So that's one thing.

14:43.826 --> 14:45.286 align:middle
In addition, in this cycle,

14:45.496 --> 14:48.886 align:middle
longer-run inflation
expectations have generally been

14:49.296 --> 14:53.576 align:middle
fairly well anchored, and, as
I've said, there's no basis

14:53.576 --> 14:54.526 align:middle
for complacency there.

14:54.526 --> 14:57.876 align:middle
But to the extent that
continues to be the case,

14:57.876 --> 14:59.986 align:middle
that should make it easier
to restore price stability.

15:00.666 --> 15:02.346 align:middle
And I guess the third
thing I would point

15:02.346 --> 15:04.966 align:middle
to that's different
this time is that part

15:04.966 --> 15:07.886 align:middle
of this inflation is caused by
this series of supply shocks

15:07.886 --> 15:10.416 align:middle
that we've had, beginning with
the pandemic, and, really,

15:10.416 --> 15:12.216 align:middle
with the reopening
of the economy,

15:12.716 --> 15:16.416 align:middle
and more recently amplified and
added to by Russia's invasion

15:16.416 --> 15:18.206 align:middle
of Ukraine-have all contributed

15:18.206 --> 15:20.236 align:middle
to the sharp increase
in inflation.

15:20.236 --> 15:23.426 align:middle
So these are the kinds of
events that are not really seen

15:23.426 --> 15:26.686 align:middle
in prior business
cycles, and, in principle,

15:26.686 --> 15:28.716 align:middle
if those things start

15:28.716 --> 15:30.966 align:middle
to get better-and we
do see some evidence

15:30.966 --> 15:32.006 align:middle
of the beginnings of that.

15:32.336 --> 15:34.746 align:middle
It's not much more than that,
but it's good to see that.

15:35.326 --> 15:38.096 align:middle
For example, commodity prices
look like they may have peaked

15:38.096 --> 15:40.306 align:middle
for now; supply chain
disruptions are beginning

15:40.306 --> 15:40.776 align:middle
to resolve.

15:41.056 --> 15:42.846 align:middle
Those developments,
if sustained,

15:42.846 --> 15:44.506 align:middle
could help ease the
pressures on inflation.

15:44.906 --> 15:49.996 align:middle
So let me just say, how
much these factors will turn

15:49.996 --> 15:52.196 align:middle
out to really matter
in this sequence

15:52.256 --> 15:54.206 align:middle
of events-it remains to be seen.

15:54.656 --> 15:55.876 align:middle
We have always understood

15:56.206 --> 15:58.926 align:middle
that restoring price stability
while achieving a relatively

15:58.926 --> 16:02.196 align:middle
modest decline-or, rather,
increase-in unemployment

16:02.706 --> 16:04.846 align:middle
and a soft landing would
be very challenging.

16:05.176 --> 16:08.436 align:middle
And we don't know-no one knows
whether this process will lead

16:08.436 --> 16:10.856 align:middle
to a recession or, if
so, how significant

16:10.856 --> 16:11.876 align:middle
that recession would be.

16:12.266 --> 16:15.146 align:middle
That's going to depend
on how quickly wage

16:15.146 --> 16:17.716 align:middle
and price inflation
pressures come down,

16:17.936 --> 16:22.246 align:middle
whether expectations remain
anchored, and whether, also,

16:22.246 --> 16:24.316 align:middle
do we get more labor supply,
which would help as well.

16:24.756 --> 16:28.116 align:middle
In addition, the chances of
a soft landing are likely

16:28.116 --> 16:30.086 align:middle
to diminish to the
extent that policy needs

16:30.086 --> 16:33.266 align:middle
to be more restrictive or
restrictive for longer.

16:34.066 --> 16:36.606 align:middle
Nonetheless, we're committed
to getting inflation back

16:36.606 --> 16:39.416 align:middle
down to 2 percent-because
we think that a failure

16:39.416 --> 16:42.726 align:middle
to restore price stability would
mean far greater pain later on.

16:42.726 --> 16:43.536 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

16:43.826 --> 16:46.566 align:middle
Just to follow up on one small
thing-are vacancies still

16:46.566 --> 16:47.846 align:middle
at the top of your list in terms

16:47.846 --> 16:49.146 align:middle
of understanding
the labor market

16:49.146 --> 16:50.566 align:middle
and how much room
there is there?

16:51.226 --> 16:52.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:52.026 --> 16:55.586 align:middle
Yes, vacancies are still
almost a 2-to-1 ratio

16:55.586 --> 16:56.956 align:middle
to unemployed people.

16:57.566 --> 17:01.776 align:middle
That and quits are really
very good ways to look

17:01.776 --> 17:03.926 align:middle
at how tight the labor market
is and how different it is

17:03.926 --> 17:07.086 align:middle
from other cycles,
where, generally,

17:07.236 --> 17:10.096 align:middle
the unemployment rate itself
is the single best indicator.

17:10.096 --> 17:13.646 align:middle
We think those things
have, for quite a time now,

17:13.746 --> 17:15.606 align:middle
really added value in
terms of understanding

17:15.606 --> 17:17.066 align:middle
where the labor market is.

17:17.976 --> 17:18.486 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

17:19.516 --> 17:21.966 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

17:23.676 --> 17:24.956 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

17:25.446 --> 17:26.856 align:middle
You said not too long ago,

17:27.216 --> 17:29.686 align:middle
in describing the
policy destination,

17:30.146 --> 17:31.706 align:middle
there's still a way to go.

17:32.016 --> 17:34.486 align:middle
But I imagine you
have to have some idea

17:34.486 --> 17:37.006 align:middle
about how you're thinking
about your destination,

17:37.006 --> 17:39.346 align:middle
whether it's a stopping
point or a pausing point.

17:39.856 --> 17:40.926 align:middle
And so I was wondering

17:40.926 --> 17:44.546 align:middle
if you could discuss how
you are thinking about,

17:44.736 --> 17:48.266 align:middle
as the data come in,
where that destination is,

17:48.266 --> 17:49.306 align:middle
how it's moving up.

17:49.306 --> 17:52.216 align:middle
If inflation doesn't
perform as you expect,

17:52.686 --> 17:54.896 align:middle
do you want to have
a policy rate that's

17:54.896 --> 17:59.016 align:middle
above the underlying
inflation rate, for example,

17:59.376 --> 18:00.526 align:middle
and do you have an estimate

18:00.526 --> 18:03.376 align:middle
for where you think the
underlying inflation rate might

18:03.376 --> 18:04.846 align:middle
be in the economy right now?

18:05.386 --> 18:06.366 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:06.586 --> 18:10.756 align:middle
Well, so, again, we
believe that we need

18:10.756 --> 18:14.826 align:middle
to raise our policy
stance overall to a level

18:14.826 --> 18:19.816 align:middle
that is restrictive-and by
that I mean, is meaningfully,

18:20.116 --> 18:23.536 align:middle
putting meaningful downward
pressure on inflation.

18:23.706 --> 18:28.146 align:middle
That's what we need to see
in the stance of policy.

18:28.206 --> 18:31.356 align:middle
We also know that there are long
and variable lags, particularly

18:31.356 --> 18:32.486 align:middle
as they relate to inflation.

18:32.486 --> 18:34.636 align:middle
So it's a challenging
assessment.

18:35.136 --> 18:36.016 align:middle
So, what do you look at?

18:36.016 --> 18:38.266 align:middle
You look at broader financial
conditions, as you know;

18:38.266 --> 18:42.446 align:middle
you look at where rates are,
real and nominal in some cases;

18:42.446 --> 18:43.356 align:middle
you look at credit spreads;

18:43.356 --> 18:46.446 align:middle
you look at financial
conditions indexes.

18:46.836 --> 18:49.196 align:middle
We also, I would
think-and you see this

18:49.196 --> 18:50.996 align:middle
in the-this is something
we talked about today

18:50.996 --> 18:53.396 align:middle
in the meeting and talk
about in all of our meetings.

18:53.396 --> 18:55.596 align:middle
And you see this, I think,
in the Committee forecast.

18:55.596 --> 18:58.546 align:middle
You want to be at a place
where real rates are positive

18:58.546 --> 18:59.946 align:middle
across the entire yield curve.

19:00.396 --> 19:03.456 align:middle
And I think that would be the
case if you look at the numbers

19:03.456 --> 19:09.396 align:middle
that we're writing down and
think about-you measure those

19:09.396 --> 19:11.776 align:middle
against some sort of
forward-looking assessment

19:11.776 --> 19:13.946 align:middle
of inflation, inflation
expectations.

19:13.946 --> 19:17.656 align:middle
I think you would see at that
time-you'd see positive real

19:17.656 --> 19:20.176 align:middle
rates across the yield curve,

19:20.176 --> 19:21.976 align:middle
and that is also an
important consideration.

19:22.466 --> 19:23.566 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:24.526 --> 19:28.046 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

19:28.046 --> 19:29.736 align:middle
Hi. Howard Schneider
with Reuters.

19:29.736 --> 19:31.786 align:middle
Thanks for the opportunity.

19:32.106 --> 19:34.396 align:middle
I just want to be
clear on the SEPs.

19:34.936 --> 19:38.636 align:middle
You say it's meeting by
meeting, but it sure looks

19:38.636 --> 19:41.706 align:middle
like we're going 75-50-25.

19:42.126 --> 19:45.006 align:middle
Is 75 next month the baseline?

19:45.256 --> 19:46.866 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:47.676 --> 19:50.336 align:middle
So we make one decision
per meeting,

19:50.336 --> 19:52.386 align:middle
and the decision
we made today was

19:52.386 --> 19:55.016 align:middle
to raise the federal funds
rate by 75 [basis points].

19:56.726 --> 20:00.966 align:middle
You're right that the median

20:00.966 --> 20:05.606 align:middle
for the year-end suggests
another 125 basis points

20:05.606 --> 20:08.556 align:middle
in rate increases,
but there's also,

20:08.556 --> 20:10.776 align:middle
there's another fairly
large group

20:10.776 --> 20:14.476 align:middle
that saw a 100 basis points
addition to where we are today.

20:14.926 --> 20:16.976 align:middle
So that would be 25
basis points less.

20:16.976 --> 20:19.676 align:middle
So we're going to make that
decision at the meeting.

20:19.676 --> 20:22.616 align:middle
We didn't make that decision
today; we didn't vote on that.

20:22.616 --> 20:25.026 align:middle
I would say that we're
committed to getting

20:25.026 --> 20:30.146 align:middle
to a restrictive level
for the federal funds rate

20:30.256 --> 20:32.006 align:middle
and getting there
pretty quickly,

20:32.576 --> 20:34.326 align:middle
and that's what we're
thinking about.

20:34.886 --> 20:35.616 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

20:35.616 --> 20:37.306 align:middle
So, just as a follow-up to that,
I'm wondering about the sort

20:37.306 --> 20:39.086 align:middle
of risk-management
considerations here,

20:39.166 --> 20:42.136 align:middle
given there's some
discussion now of overdoing it.

20:42.136 --> 20:46.346 align:middle
What's the incentive to continue
front-loading right now?

20:46.536 --> 20:48.186 align:middle
Is it lack of progress

20:48.186 --> 20:50.436 align:middle
on inflation seen
in the CPI reports?

20:50.866 --> 20:53.406 align:middle
Or is it a motivation to get

20:53.406 --> 20:56.076 align:middle
as much done while the
job market is still

20:56.076 --> 20:56.676 align:middle
as strong as it is?

20:56.676 --> 20:57.066 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:57.336 --> 20:58.826 align:middle
So, what we've seen is,

20:59.306 --> 21:03.016 align:middle
inflation has-our expectation
has been that we would begin

21:03.016 --> 21:04.166 align:middle
to see inflation come down,

21:04.166 --> 21:06.946 align:middle
largely because of
supply-side healing.

21:07.056 --> 21:08.216 align:middle
By now we would have thought

21:08.216 --> 21:09.736 align:middle
that we'd have seen
some of that.

21:10.166 --> 21:10.576 align:middle
We haven't.

21:10.576 --> 21:12.136 align:middle
We have seen some
supply-side healing,

21:12.136 --> 21:14.146 align:middle
but inflation has
not really come down.

21:14.506 --> 21:16.896 align:middle
If you look at core
PCE inflation,

21:16.896 --> 21:20.446 align:middle
which is a good measure of
where inflation is running now,

21:20.926 --> 21:23.176 align:middle
if you look at it on a 3-, 6-,

21:23.226 --> 21:25.616 align:middle
and 12-month trailing
annualized basis,

21:25.616 --> 21:29.026 align:middle
you'll see that inflation
is at 4.8 percent,

21:29.506 --> 21:31.576 align:middle
4.5 percent, and 4.8 percent.

21:32.116 --> 21:35.266 align:middle
So that's a pretty good summary
of where we are with inflation.

21:35.626 --> 21:37.746 align:middle
And that's not where we
expected or wanted to be.

21:38.066 --> 21:41.726 align:middle
So, what that tells us is
that we need to continue,

21:41.726 --> 21:43.636 align:middle
and we can-keep doing these,

21:43.636 --> 21:46.666 align:middle
and we did today-do
another large increase

21:46.956 --> 21:50.136 align:middle
as we approach the level that
we think we need to get to.

21:50.236 --> 21:52.086 align:middle
And we're still discovering
what that level is.

21:52.086 --> 21:54.706 align:middle
But people are writing
that down in their SEP

21:55.206 --> 21:56.886 align:middle
where they think
policy needs to be.

21:56.886 --> 21:58.396 align:middle
So that's how we're
thinking about it.

21:58.396 --> 21:59.086 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

22:00.336 --> 22:01.796 align:middle
Let's go to Colby.

22:02.226 --> 22:02.516 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

22:05.356 --> 22:05.696 align:middle
Thank you.

22:05.696 --> 22:07.846 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the Financial Times.

22:07.846 --> 22:09.746 align:middle
Chair Powell, how should
we interpret the fact

22:09.746 --> 22:13.446 align:middle
that core inflation is still not
forecast in the SEP to be back

22:13.566 --> 22:18.086 align:middle
to target in 2025, and yet
the dot plot projects cuts

22:18.086 --> 22:19.536 align:middle
as early as 2024?

22:19.536 --> 22:22.706 align:middle
And does that mean there's
a level of inflation

22:23.076 --> 22:27.836 align:middle
above the 2 percent target that
the Fed is willing to tolerate?

22:28.526 --> 22:31.056 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:31.246 --> 22:33.246 align:middle
So I guess core is at 2.1

22:33.246 --> 22:39.116 align:middle
in 2025-in the median-and
headline is at 2.0,

22:39.116 --> 22:40.166 align:middle
so that's pretty close.

22:40.776 --> 22:44.146 align:middle
I mean, we write down our
forecasts, and we figure

22:44.466 --> 22:46.316 align:middle
out what the median
is, and we publish it.

22:46.396 --> 22:51.946 align:middle
So it's not-I mean, I would
say that if, actually,

22:51.946 --> 22:53.266 align:middle
if the economy followed
this path,

22:53.296 --> 22:54.416 align:middle
this would be a pretty
good outcome.

22:54.746 --> 22:56.406 align:middle
But you're right-it is a
tenth higher than 2 percent.

22:56.406 --> 22:57.256 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

22:57.256 --> 22:59.656 align:middle
Okay. Just as a quick follow-up:
I mean, if the concern is

22:59.656 --> 23:02.896 align:middle
that underlying inflation
is becoming more entrenched,

23:02.896 --> 23:07.636 align:middle
perhaps, each month, then why
forgo the more aggressive 100

23:07.636 --> 23:09.336 align:middle
basis point increase today,

23:09.626 --> 23:11.466 align:middle
and does that risk having
to do more later on?

23:11.916 --> 23:12.346 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:12.456 --> 23:16.186 align:middle
Yeah. So we, as we said at the
last press conference and in

23:16.186 --> 23:18.346 align:middle
between that one and this one,

23:18.346 --> 23:20.566 align:middle
we said that we would
make our decision based

23:20.566 --> 23:22.036 align:middle
on the overall data coming in.

23:22.036 --> 23:26.476 align:middle
So, if you remember, we got a
surprisingly low reading in July

23:26.476 --> 23:30.136 align:middle
and then a surprisingly
high reading for August.

23:30.136 --> 23:32.916 align:middle
So I think you have to-you
can't really-you never want

23:32.916 --> 23:35.116 align:middle
to overreact too much
to any one data point.

23:35.636 --> 23:37.456 align:middle
So if you look at
them together-and,

23:37.456 --> 23:39.166 align:middle
as I just mentioned,
if you really look

23:39.166 --> 23:42.406 align:middle
at this year's inflation, 3-,
6-, and 12-month trailing,

23:42.406 --> 23:44.406 align:middle
you see inflation
is running too high.

23:44.626 --> 23:48.086 align:middle
It's running 4.5 percent
or above; you don't need

23:48.086 --> 23:49.076 align:middle
to know much more than that.

23:49.226 --> 23:50.476 align:middle
If that's the one
thing you know,

23:50.476 --> 23:53.746 align:middle
you know that this Committee
is committed to getting

23:53.746 --> 23:56.616 align:middle
to a meaningfully
restrictive stance of policy

23:56.816 --> 24:00.206 align:middle
and staying there
until we feel confident

24:00.206 --> 24:01.386 align:middle
that inflation is coming down.

24:01.826 --> 24:02.956 align:middle
That's how we think about it.

24:02.956 --> 24:04.056 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

24:04.696 --> 24:06.006 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

24:09.106 --> 24:10.916 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

24:10.976 --> 24:13.846 align:middle
I wanted to ask about
the balance sheet.

24:14.026 --> 24:16.286 align:middle
You all have left
open the possibility

24:16.286 --> 24:18.486 align:middle
that you might sell
mortgage-backed securities,

24:18.526 --> 24:21.456 align:middle
but we've seen significant
slowing in the housing market.

24:21.456 --> 24:23.076 align:middle
Mortgage rates have
gone up significantly,

24:23.076 --> 24:27.556 align:middle
and I'm just wondering whether
conditions there might affect

24:27.556 --> 24:30.766 align:middle
your plans for how quickly you
have the runoff on the MBS side.

24:31.166 --> 24:31.696 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:31.696 --> 24:35.806 align:middle
So we, what we said, as you
know, was that we would consider

24:35.806 --> 24:40.146 align:middle
that once balance sheet
runoff is well under way.

24:40.296 --> 24:42.386 align:middle
I would say it's not something
we're considering right now

24:42.386 --> 24:45.826 align:middle
and not something I expect to
be considering in the near term.

24:46.136 --> 24:48.606 align:middle
It's just, it's something
I think we will turn to,

24:48.606 --> 24:50.736 align:middle
but that time, the
time for turning to it,

24:51.426 --> 24:52.906 align:middle
has not come and is not close.

24:53.166 --> 24:53.826 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

24:53.826 --> 24:56.086 align:middle
Will conditions in the housing
market affect that decision?

24:56.086 --> 24:56.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:56.296 --> 24:59.246 align:middle
I think a number of things might
affect that decision, really.

24:59.246 --> 25:01.236 align:middle
The main thing is, we're not
considering that decision,

25:01.236 --> 25:03.106 align:middle
and I don't expect that
we will any time soon.

25:03.316 --> 25:04.056 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

25:04.606 --> 25:04.986 align:middle
Thank you.

25:05.106 --> 25:06.636 align:middle
Neil. NEIL IRWIN.

25:07.346 --> 25:09.636 align:middle
Thanks. Neil Irwin with Axios.

25:09.636 --> 25:13.666 align:middle
A number of commentators
have come to the view,

25:13.666 --> 25:14.946 align:middle
including over at
the World Bank,

25:14.946 --> 25:17.866 align:middle
that simultaneous global
tightening around the world is,

25:18.226 --> 25:19.966 align:middle
creates a risk of a global
recession that's worse

25:19.996 --> 25:21.546 align:middle
than is necessary to
bring inflation down.

25:21.906 --> 25:23.466 align:middle
How do you see that risk?

25:23.466 --> 25:25.166 align:middle
How do you think of coordination

25:25.166 --> 25:26.516 align:middle
with your fellow
central bankers?

25:26.846 --> 25:31.066 align:middle
And is there much risk of
overdoing it on a global level?

25:31.176 --> 25:31.386 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:31.866 --> 25:34.826 align:middle
So we, actually, my
colleagues and I,

25:34.826 --> 25:37.966 align:middle
a number of my FOMC colleagues
and I, just got back from one

25:37.966 --> 25:41.546 align:middle
of our frequent trips
to Basel, Switzerland,

25:41.546 --> 25:44.076 align:middle
to meet with other senior
central bank officials

25:44.076 --> 25:44.856 align:middle
from around the world.

25:45.376 --> 25:48.026 align:middle
We are in pretty regular
contact, and we exchange,

25:48.026 --> 25:49.956 align:middle
of course-we all serve
a domestic mandate,

25:50.026 --> 25:52.126 align:middle
domestic objectives-in our
case, the dual mandate,

25:52.126 --> 25:53.446 align:middle
maximum employment
and price stability.

25:54.016 --> 25:58.296 align:middle
But we regularly discuss
what we're seeing in terms

25:58.296 --> 26:00.376 align:middle
of our own economy and
international spillovers,

26:00.376 --> 26:03.666 align:middle
and it's a very ongoing,
constant kind of a process.

26:04.226 --> 26:09.176 align:middle
So we are very aware of what's
going on in other economies

26:09.176 --> 26:11.676 align:middle
around the world and what that
means for us, and vice versa.

26:12.456 --> 26:15.626 align:middle
The forecasts that
we put together,

26:15.626 --> 26:17.636 align:middle
that our staff puts together,
and that we put together

26:17.636 --> 26:19.816 align:middle
on our own always
take all of that-try

26:19.816 --> 26:20.906 align:middle
to take all of that
into account.

26:20.906 --> 26:23.176 align:middle
I mean, I can't say that we
do it perfectly, but it's not

26:23.586 --> 26:28.396 align:middle
as if we don't think about the
policy decisions-monetary policy

26:28.396 --> 26:30.186 align:middle
and otherwise-the
economic developments

26:30.456 --> 26:32.476 align:middle
that are taking place
in major economies

26:32.476 --> 26:33.826 align:middle
that can have an effect
on the U.S. economy.

26:34.066 --> 26:38.176 align:middle
That is very much baked
into our own forecast

26:38.176 --> 26:42.146 align:middle
and our own understanding of the
U.S. economy, as best we can.

26:42.176 --> 26:43.096 align:middle
It won't be perfect.

26:43.826 --> 26:49.966 align:middle
So I don't see-it's hard to talk
about collaboration in a world

26:49.966 --> 26:52.796 align:middle
where people have very different
levels of interest rates.

26:52.796 --> 26:55.546 align:middle
If you remember, there were
coordinated cuts and raises

26:55.546 --> 26:58.566 align:middle
and things like that at various
times, but, really, we're all,

26:58.566 --> 27:00.136 align:middle
we're in very different
situations.

27:00.616 --> 27:04.596 align:middle
But I will tell you, our
contact is more or less ongoing,

27:05.216 --> 27:07.436 align:middle
and it's not coordination,
but there's a lot

27:07.436 --> 27:08.556 align:middle
of information sharing.

27:08.556 --> 27:10.626 align:middle
And we all, I think,
are informed

27:10.736 --> 27:14.136 align:middle
by what other important
economies-economies

27:14.136 --> 27:17.066 align:middle
that are important to the
United States-are doing.

27:17.066 --> 27:18.486 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:18.796 --> 27:21.546 align:middle
Craig. CRAIG TORRES.

27:22.096 --> 27:23.376 align:middle
Craig Torres from Bloomberg.

27:23.806 --> 27:24.886 align:middle
Chair Powell, you talked

27:24.886 --> 27:28.406 align:middle
about some ways the higher
interest rates are affecting the

27:28.406 --> 27:32.716 align:middle
economy, but we've also seen
a resilient labor market

27:32.926 --> 27:36.666 align:middle
with durable consumption,
strong corporate profits,

27:37.206 --> 27:39.426 align:middle
and I'm wondering
what your story is

27:39.426 --> 27:41.776 align:middle
on the resilience
of the economy.

27:41.776 --> 27:43.706 align:middle
After all, you and your
colleagues said, "Well,

27:43.706 --> 27:45.506 align:middle
we started tightening in
March when we were talking

27:45.506 --> 27:47.526 align:middle
about interest rates
in the future."

27:47.636 --> 27:50.096 align:middle
And, indeed, Treasury
rates moved up,

27:50.096 --> 27:54.676 align:middle
so we should have had a lot
of tightening taking effect.

27:55.126 --> 27:59.166 align:middle
Why is the economy, in
your view, so resilient,

27:59.196 --> 28:01.876 align:middle
and does it mean that we
might need a possibly higher

28:01.936 --> 28:02.536 align:middle
terminal rate?

28:03.296 --> 28:03.666 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:03.746 --> 28:05.386 align:middle
You're right, of
course-the labor market,

28:05.386 --> 28:07.716 align:middle
in particular, has
been very strong.

28:07.936 --> 28:12.316 align:middle
But there are-the
sectors of the economy

28:12.316 --> 28:17.566 align:middle
that are most interest rate
sensitive are certainly showing

28:17.566 --> 28:19.026 align:middle
the effects of our
tightening, and, of course,

28:19.086 --> 28:23.976 align:middle
the obvious example is housing,
where you see declining activity

28:23.976 --> 28:28.046 align:middle
of all different kinds and house
price increases moving down.

28:28.406 --> 28:33.076 align:middle
So we're having an effect on
interest-sensitive spending.

28:33.726 --> 28:37.676 align:middle
I think, through exchange
rates, we're having an effect

28:37.676 --> 28:40.026 align:middle
on exports and imports.

28:40.806 --> 28:46.496 align:middle
I think-so all of that's
happening, but you're right,

28:46.496 --> 28:50.546 align:middle
and we've said this: This
is a strong, robust economy.

28:51.036 --> 28:54.866 align:middle
People have savings on their
balance sheet from the period

28:54.866 --> 28:55.936 align:middle
when they couldn't spend

28:56.026 --> 28:57.726 align:middle
and where they were getting
government transfers.

28:57.726 --> 29:00.956 align:middle
There's still very significant
savings out there, although not

29:00.956 --> 29:03.836 align:middle
as much at the lower end of
the income spectrum-but still,

29:04.036 --> 29:07.166 align:middle
some savings out there
to support growth.

29:07.166 --> 29:11.776 align:middle
The states are very flush with
cash, so there's good reason

29:11.776 --> 29:13.396 align:middle
to think that this will continue

29:13.396 --> 29:15.956 align:middle
to be a reasonably
strong economy.

29:16.096 --> 29:21.406 align:middle
Now, the data sort of are
showing that growth is going

29:21.886 --> 29:23.446 align:middle
to be below trend this year.

29:23.446 --> 29:27.206 align:middle
We think of trend as being about
1.8 percent, or in that range.

29:27.786 --> 29:30.476 align:middle
We're forecasting
growth well below that,

29:30.476 --> 29:31.816 align:middle
and most forecasters are.

29:32.426 --> 29:35.816 align:middle
But you're right-there's
certainly a possibility

29:36.016 --> 29:37.616 align:middle
that growth can be
stronger than that.

29:38.296 --> 29:41.686 align:middle
And that's a good thing, because
that means the economy will be

29:41.686 --> 29:45.676 align:middle
more resistant to a
significant downturn.

29:46.336 --> 29:51.046 align:middle
But, of course, we are focused
on the thing I started with,

29:51.116 --> 29:54.396 align:middle
which is getting inflation
back down to 2 percent.

29:54.966 --> 29:57.746 align:middle
We can't fail to do that.

29:57.746 --> 30:01.016 align:middle
I mean, if we were to fail to
do that, that would be the thing

30:01.016 --> 30:03.256 align:middle
that would be most painful
for the people that we serve.

30:03.836 --> 30:08.376 align:middle
So for now, that has to
be our overarching focus,

30:09.066 --> 30:13.696 align:middle
and you see that, I think, in
the SEP, in the levels of rates

30:13.736 --> 30:15.936 align:middle
that we'll be moving
to reasonably quickly,

30:16.636 --> 30:19.606 align:middle
assuming things turn out
roughly in line with the SEP.

30:20.646 --> 30:21.646 align:middle
So that's how we think about it.

30:22.956 --> 30:23.786 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

30:23.856 --> 30:25.766 align:middle
Mike. Thank you.

30:26.056 --> 30:26.686 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

30:27.006 --> 30:28.116 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

30:28.116 --> 30:31.046 align:middle
In a world of euphemisms
that we live in here,

30:31.046 --> 30:34.156 align:middle
with "below-trend growth"
and "modest increase

30:34.156 --> 30:35.976 align:middle
in unemployment," I'm wondering
if I can ask you a couple

30:35.976 --> 30:37.816 align:middle
of direct questions for
the American people.

30:38.486 --> 30:41.216 align:middle
Do the odds now favor,
given where you are

30:41.216 --> 30:41.886 align:middle
and where you're going

30:41.886 --> 30:43.906 align:middle
with interest rates,
favor a recession?

30:44.486 --> 30:46.966 align:middle
Four point four percent
unemployment is

30:46.966 --> 30:49.246 align:middle
about 1.3 million jobs.

30:49.246 --> 30:51.106 align:middle
Is that acceptable job loss?

30:51.106 --> 30:55.186 align:middle
And then, given that the data
you look at is backward looking,

30:55.186 --> 30:57.886 align:middle
and the lags in your
policy are forward looking,

30:57.886 --> 31:00.566 align:middle
and you don't know what
they are, how will you know,

31:00.706 --> 31:03.206 align:middle
or will you know, if
you've gone too far?

31:04.376 --> 31:05.486 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:05.576 --> 31:07.996 align:middle
So I don't know what
the odds are.

31:08.246 --> 31:11.146 align:middle
I think that there's
a very high likelihood

31:11.786 --> 31:17.126 align:middle
that we'll have a period of what
I've mentioned is below-trend

31:17.126 --> 31:19.056 align:middle
growth, by which I
mean much lower growth,

31:19.056 --> 31:20.116 align:middle
and we're seeing that now.

31:20.536 --> 31:24.286 align:middle
So the median forecast, I think,
this year among my colleagues

31:24.286 --> 31:26.596 align:middle
and me was 0.2 percent growth.

31:26.596 --> 31:28.106 align:middle
So that's very slow growth.

31:28.166 --> 31:29.906 align:middle
And then below trend next year.

31:29.906 --> 31:32.566 align:middle
I think the median was
1.2, also well below.

31:32.566 --> 31:37.066 align:middle
So that's a slower, that's
a very slow level of growth,

31:37.146 --> 31:39.606 align:middle
and it could give rise to
increases in unemployment.

31:40.186 --> 31:43.456 align:middle
But I think that's, so that is
something that we think we need

31:43.456 --> 31:44.696 align:middle
to have, and we think we need

31:44.696 --> 31:46.946 align:middle
to have softer labor
market conditions as well.

31:47.736 --> 31:49.646 align:middle
We're never going to say

31:49.646 --> 31:51.956 align:middle
that there are too
many people working,

31:52.436 --> 31:56.446 align:middle
but the real point is this:
Inflation-what we hear

31:56.446 --> 31:58.236 align:middle
from people when we
meet with them is

31:58.716 --> 32:01.366 align:middle
that they really are
suffering from inflation.

32:01.676 --> 32:05.366 align:middle
And if we want to set ourselves
up, really light the way

32:05.366 --> 32:08.656 align:middle
to another period of a
very strong labor market,

32:08.656 --> 32:11.156 align:middle
we have got to get
inflation behind us.

32:11.626 --> 32:13.866 align:middle
I wish there were a
painless way to do that.

32:14.096 --> 32:14.606 align:middle
There isn't.

32:15.186 --> 32:18.186 align:middle
So, what we need to do is
get rates up to the point

32:18.186 --> 32:21.886 align:middle
where we're putting meaningful
downward pressure on inflation,

32:22.646 --> 32:24.236 align:middle
and that's what we're doing.

32:24.466 --> 32:28.906 align:middle
And we certainly don't hope,
we certainly haven't given

32:28.906 --> 32:32.196 align:middle
up the idea that we can have
a relatively modest increase

32:32.726 --> 32:33.776 align:middle
in unemployment.

32:34.296 --> 32:36.326 align:middle
Nonetheless, we need
to complete this task.

32:36.986 --> 32:37.576 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

32:37.576 --> 32:40.206 align:middle
But how will you know,
or will you know,

32:40.206 --> 32:42.076 align:middle
if you've gone too far?

32:42.076 --> 32:42.586 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:42.586 --> 32:45.506 align:middle
It's hard to hypothetically
deal with that question.

32:45.506 --> 32:48.806 align:middle
I mean, again, our really
tight focus now continues

32:48.806 --> 32:51.876 align:middle
to be ongoing rate increases
to get the policy rate

32:51.946 --> 32:53.646 align:middle
up where it needs to be.

32:54.186 --> 32:59.266 align:middle
And, as I said, you can look
at this SEP as today's estimate

32:59.406 --> 33:00.816 align:middle
of where we think
those rates would be.

33:00.816 --> 33:02.536 align:middle
Of course, they will
evolve over time.

33:03.616 --> 33:03.996 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:04.176 --> 33:04.546 align:middle
Chris Rugaber.

33:05.436 --> 33:06.766 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

33:06.806 --> 33:08.496 align:middle
Thanks. Chris Rugaber,
Associated Press.

33:08.836 --> 33:11.196 align:middle
I wanted to follow up with
what you just mentioned

33:11.196 --> 33:12.006 align:middle
about the labor market.

33:12.006 --> 33:13.906 align:middle
You've said several times

33:13.946 --> 33:17.556 align:middle
that to have the labor market we
want, we need price stability,

33:18.106 --> 33:20.726 align:middle
and you suggested maybe
there isn't a tradeoff

33:20.726 --> 33:21.376 align:middle
in the long run.

33:22.146 --> 33:23.856 align:middle
But in the short run,
there is a lot of concern,

33:23.986 --> 33:26.836 align:middle
as people have been expressing
here, about higher unemployment

33:26.836 --> 33:30.716 align:middle
as a result of these rate hikes
or as a result of rate hikes.

33:30.766 --> 33:31.986 align:middle
So can you explain, though,

33:32.306 --> 33:36.906 align:middle
what about high inflation
now threatens the job market?

33:36.906 --> 33:41.496 align:middle
I mean, you seem to suggest
inflation, high inflation,

33:41.926 --> 33:45.156 align:middle
will eventually lead
to a weaker job market.

33:45.256 --> 33:46.726 align:middle
So can you spell that
out a little more

33:46.726 --> 33:48.326 align:middle
for the general public
on how that would work?

33:48.326 --> 33:48.536 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:48.536 --> 33:52.156 align:middle
So, for starters, people are
seeing their wage increases

33:52.486 --> 33:54.246 align:middle
eaten up by inflation.

33:55.036 --> 33:59.606 align:middle
So if your family is one where
you spend most of your paycheck,

33:59.866 --> 34:04.776 align:middle
every paycheck cycle, on gas,
food, transportation, clothing,

34:04.896 --> 34:08.716 align:middle
basics of life, and prices go up
the way they've been going up,

34:08.906 --> 34:09.926 align:middle
you're in trouble right away.

34:10.406 --> 34:14.366 align:middle
You don't have a cushion, and
this is very painful for people

34:14.486 --> 34:16.366 align:middle
at the lower end of the
income and wealth spectrum.

34:16.366 --> 34:18.976 align:middle
So that's what we're hearing
from people is, you know,

34:18.976 --> 34:21.946 align:middle
very much-that inflation
is really hurting.

34:22.486 --> 34:23.796 align:middle
So how do we get
rid of inflation?

34:23.996 --> 34:27.496 align:middle
And, as I mentioned, it would
be nice if there were a way

34:27.496 --> 34:30.166 align:middle
to just wish it away,
but there isn't.

34:30.256 --> 34:33.806 align:middle
We have to get supply and
demand back into alignment,

34:33.806 --> 34:36.196 align:middle
and the way we do that is
by slowing the economy.

34:36.456 --> 34:38.566 align:middle
Hopefully, we do that
by slowing the economy,

34:39.146 --> 34:42.186 align:middle
and we see some softening
in labor market conditions,

34:42.186 --> 34:44.726 align:middle
and we see a big contribution

34:44.726 --> 34:48.466 align:middle
from supply-side improvements,
and things like that.

34:48.506 --> 34:49.816 align:middle
But none of that is guaranteed.

34:50.456 --> 34:53.706 align:middle
In any case, we, our job is
to deliver price stability,

34:53.766 --> 34:57.446 align:middle
and I think-you can think of
price stability as an asset

34:57.446 --> 35:00.766 align:middle
that just delivers large
benefits to society

35:00.766 --> 35:02.266 align:middle
over a long period of time.

35:02.266 --> 35:04.596 align:middle
We really saw that
for a long time.

35:04.596 --> 35:07.136 align:middle
The United States had
2 percent inflation,

35:07.136 --> 35:10.276 align:middle
didn't move around much, and
that was enormously beneficial

35:10.276 --> 35:12.246 align:middle
to the public that we serve.

35:13.006 --> 35:15.396 align:middle
And we have to get back
to that and keep it

35:15.396 --> 35:17.186 align:middle
for another long period of time.

35:17.506 --> 35:21.056 align:middle
To pull back from the
task of doing that is,

35:21.166 --> 35:23.336 align:middle
you're just postponing-the
record shows

35:23.336 --> 35:24.566 align:middle
that if you postpone that,

35:24.566 --> 35:28.596 align:middle
the delay is only likely
to lead to more pain.

35:29.096 --> 35:33.816 align:middle
So I think we're moving
to do what we need to do

35:33.816 --> 35:36.816 align:middle
and do our jobs, and that's
what you see us doing.

35:36.816 --> 35:38.386 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

35:39.386 --> 35:40.786 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

35:41.426 --> 35:43.156 align:middle
Thank you for taking the
question, Mr. Chairman.

35:43.156 --> 35:44.666 align:middle
Edward Lawrence for
Fox Business.

35:44.836 --> 35:47.326 align:middle
So you had said that
Americans and businesses need

35:47.326 --> 35:49.456 align:middle
to feel some economic
pain as we go forward.

35:49.456 --> 35:52.076 align:middle
How long from here should
Americans be prepared

35:52.346 --> 35:53.226 align:middle
for that economic pain?

35:53.226 --> 35:54.476 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:54.476 --> 35:57.656 align:middle
How long? I mean, it really
depends on how long it takes

35:57.656 --> 36:00.876 align:middle
for wages and, more than
that, prices to come

36:00.876 --> 36:02.746 align:middle
down for inflation to come down.

36:02.746 --> 36:07.186 align:middle
And so what you see in
our projections today is

36:07.716 --> 36:12.956 align:middle
that inflation moves down
significantly over the course

36:12.956 --> 36:15.146 align:middle
of next year and then more
the next year after that.

36:15.146 --> 36:21.056 align:middle
And I think once you're on
that path, that's a good thing.

36:21.226 --> 36:22.636 align:middle
And things will start
to feel better

36:22.636 --> 36:24.756 align:middle
to people-they'll feel lower
inflation, they'll feel

36:24.756 --> 36:25.866 align:middle
that the economy is improving.

36:26.196 --> 36:29.666 align:middle
And also, if our projections
are close to right,

36:29.726 --> 36:35.056 align:middle
you'll see that the costs
in unemployment are,

36:35.366 --> 36:37.316 align:middle
they're meaningful, and they're
certainly very meaningful

36:37.316 --> 36:38.696 align:middle
to the people who
lose their jobs.

36:38.856 --> 36:42.006 align:middle
And we talk about that in
our meetings quite a lot.

36:42.196 --> 36:44.906 align:middle
But, at the same time,
we'd be setting the economy

36:44.906 --> 36:48.756 align:middle
up for another long
period-this era has been noted

36:48.756 --> 36:50.296 align:middle
for very long expansions.

36:50.296 --> 36:52.576 align:middle
We've had three of the four
longest in measured history

36:52.916 --> 36:55.426 align:middle
since we got inflation
under control.

36:55.596 --> 36:57.536 align:middle
And that's not an accident.

36:57.536 --> 37:00.186 align:middle
So when inflation is low and
stable, you can have these 9,

37:00.186 --> 37:03.756 align:middle
10, 11-10-year, anyway,
expansions,

37:03.846 --> 37:10.456 align:middle
and you can see what we
saw in 2018, '19, and '20,

37:10.456 --> 37:14.366 align:middle
which was very low unemployment,
the biggest wage gains going

37:14.366 --> 37:15.846 align:middle
to people at the low
end of the spectrum,

37:15.846 --> 37:18.616 align:middle
the smallest racial
gaps that we've seen

37:18.796 --> 37:20.546 align:middle
since we started
keeping track of that.

37:20.546 --> 37:22.316 align:middle
So we want to get back to that.

37:22.366 --> 37:25.196 align:middle
But, to get there, we're
going to have to get supply

37:25.196 --> 37:29.066 align:middle
and demand back in
alignment, and that's going

37:29.676 --> 37:33.056 align:middle
to take tight monetary
policy for a period of time.

37:33.056 --> 37:33.576 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

37:33.576 --> 37:35.866 align:middle
As a follow-up, what is that
economic pain in your mind?

37:35.866 --> 37:36.926 align:middle
Is it job losses?

37:36.926 --> 37:39.276 align:middle
Is it higher interest
rates on credit cards?

37:39.276 --> 37:40.516 align:middle
What is that economic pain?

37:40.516 --> 37:40.896 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:40.936 --> 37:42.726 align:middle
So it's all of those things.

37:42.726 --> 37:44.376 align:middle
Higher interest rates,
slower growth,

37:44.546 --> 37:47.896 align:middle
and a softening labor
market are all painful

37:47.896 --> 37:49.546 align:middle
for the public that we serve.

37:50.276 --> 37:52.216 align:middle
But they're not as
painful as failing

37:52.216 --> 37:56.546 align:middle
to restore price stability
and then having to come back

37:56.626 --> 38:01.996 align:middle
and do it down the road again
and doing it at a time when,

38:01.996 --> 38:07.396 align:middle
actually, now people have really
come to expect high inflation.

38:07.546 --> 38:11.276 align:middle
If the concept of high
inflation becomes entrenched

38:11.276 --> 38:13.586 align:middle
in people's economic thinking
about their decisions,

38:14.226 --> 38:17.836 align:middle
then sort of getting back
to price stability-the cost

38:17.836 --> 38:20.886 align:middle
of getting back to price
stability just rises,

38:21.476 --> 38:22.846 align:middle
and so we want to avoid that.

38:22.846 --> 38:26.816 align:middle
We want to act aggressively now
and get this job done and keep

38:26.816 --> 38:27.746 align:middle
at it until it's done.

38:29.286 --> 38:29.576 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:29.576 --> 38:30.146 align:middle
Nicole, from CNN.

38:30.146 --> 38:30.506 align:middle
NICOLE GOODKIND.

38:32.576 --> 38:33.436 align:middle
Thank you, Chairman Powell.

38:33.436 --> 38:34.916 align:middle
Nicole Goodkind, CNN Business.

38:35.376 --> 38:38.886 align:middle
Existing home sales have fallen
for seven months straight,

38:38.996 --> 38:41.696 align:middle
mortgage rates are at their
highest level since 2008,

38:42.096 --> 38:43.896 align:middle
yet mortgage demand
increased this week,

38:44.286 --> 38:46.086 align:middle
and housing prices
are still elevated.

38:46.376 --> 38:48.166 align:middle
Now, at the end of your
June press conference,

38:48.166 --> 38:50.596 align:middle
you mentioned plans to
reset the housing market.

38:50.596 --> 38:53.486 align:middle
I was wondering if you could
elaborate on what you mean

38:53.486 --> 38:56.766 align:middle
when you say "reset" and
what you think it will take

38:56.766 --> 38:57.546 align:middle
to actually get there.

38:58.156 --> 38:59.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:59.996 --> 39:03.106 align:middle
So when I say "reset," I'm
not looking at a particular,

39:03.106 --> 39:05.386 align:middle
specific set of data
or anything.

39:05.386 --> 39:08.156 align:middle
What I'm really saying
is that we've had a time

39:08.156 --> 39:11.906 align:middle
of a red-hot housing market
all over the country, where,

39:12.376 --> 39:17.266 align:middle
famously, houses were selling
to the first buyer at 10 percent

39:17.266 --> 39:19.716 align:middle
above the ask, before
even seeing the house.

39:19.716 --> 39:20.346 align:middle
That kind of thing.

39:20.756 --> 39:24.396 align:middle
So there was a big imbalance
between supply and demand.

39:24.396 --> 39:27.326 align:middle
Housing prices were going up
at an unsustainably fast level.

39:27.326 --> 39:29.796 align:middle
So the deceleration
in housing prices

39:29.796 --> 39:31.596 align:middle
that we're seeing
should help bring sort

39:31.596 --> 39:34.246 align:middle
of prices more closely
in line with rents

39:34.246 --> 39:35.986 align:middle
and other housing
market fundamentals,

39:36.666 --> 39:38.556 align:middle
and that's a good thing.

39:38.556 --> 39:42.166 align:middle
For the longer term, what
we need is supply and demand

39:42.166 --> 39:44.546 align:middle
to get better aligned so
that housing prices go

39:44.546 --> 39:48.446 align:middle
up at a reasonable level-at
a reasonable pace-and

39:48.446 --> 39:50.326 align:middle
that people can afford
houses again.

39:50.326 --> 39:52.736 align:middle
And I think we-so we probably,
in the housing market,

39:52.736 --> 39:55.596 align:middle
have to go through a correction
to get back to that place.

39:56.096 --> 39:57.996 align:middle
There's also, there are also
longer-run issues, though,

39:57.996 --> 39:58.906 align:middle
with the housing market.

39:58.906 --> 40:03.686 align:middle
As you know, we're-it's
difficult

40:04.166 --> 40:08.126 align:middle
to find lots now close enough
to cities and things like that,

40:08.126 --> 40:10.546 align:middle
so builders are having a hard
time getting zoning and lots

40:10.546 --> 40:12.636 align:middle
and workers and materials
and things like that.

40:12.666 --> 40:14.616 align:middle
But from a sort of
business cycle standpoint,

40:15.356 --> 40:20.056 align:middle
this difficult correction should
put the housing market back

40:20.056 --> 40:21.036 align:middle
into better balance.

40:21.036 --> 40:22.746 align:middle
NICOLE GOODKIND.

40:22.846 --> 40:27.256 align:middle
Just to follow, though, shelter
made up such a large part

40:27.256 --> 40:29.966 align:middle
of this hot CPI report
that we saw.

40:30.466 --> 40:33.216 align:middle
Do you think that there is a lag
and that we will see that come

40:33.216 --> 40:34.216 align:middle
down in the coming months?

40:34.216 --> 40:35.966 align:middle
Or do you think that
there's still this imbalance

40:35.966 --> 40:36.786 align:middle
that needs to be addressed?

40:38.126 --> 40:38.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:38.656 --> 40:40.906 align:middle
I think that shelter
inflation is going

40:40.906 --> 40:42.516 align:middle
to remain high for some time.

40:42.716 --> 40:45.656 align:middle
We're looking for it to come
down, but it's not exactly clear

40:45.656 --> 40:48.306 align:middle
when that will happen.

40:48.306 --> 40:51.936 align:middle
So it may take some
time, so I think,

40:52.076 --> 40:53.956 align:middle
hope for the best,
plan for the worst.

40:53.956 --> 40:56.586 align:middle
So I think, on shelter
inflation, you've just got

40:56.586 --> 40:58.686 align:middle
to assume that it's going to
remain pretty high for a while.

40:59.216 --> 41:00.376 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:00.376 --> 41:04.536 align:middle
Okay, we'll go to Jean
for the last question.

41:04.536 --> 41:05.086 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

41:05.226 --> 41:06.836 align:middle
Hi. Jean Yung with Market News.

41:06.916 --> 41:08.966 align:middle
You've talked about the
need to get real rates

41:08.966 --> 41:11.966 align:middle
into positive territory,
and you said earlier

41:11.966 --> 41:15.176 align:middle
that policy is just moving
into that territory now.

41:15.636 --> 41:20.126 align:middle
So I'm curious, how
restrictive is rates

41:20.126 --> 41:24.756 align:middle
at 4.6 percent expected-is
that expected to be next year?

41:25.456 --> 41:26.036 align:middle
How restrictive?

41:27.096 --> 41:27.486 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:27.486 --> 41:31.506 align:middle
So I think if you look, when we
get to-if we-let's assume we do

41:31.506 --> 41:35.346 align:middle
get to that level,
which I think is likely.

41:35.966 --> 41:38.716 align:middle
What you're going to do
is, you're going to adjust

41:38.716 --> 41:44.896 align:middle
that for some forward-looking
measure of inflation.

41:45.606 --> 41:47.566 align:middle
And that could be-you
pick your measure.

41:47.566 --> 41:50.276 align:middle
It could be-there are all kinds

41:50.276 --> 41:51.286 align:middle
of different things
you could pick

41:51.286 --> 41:53.326 align:middle
and you get-but what you'll
get is a positive number.

41:53.326 --> 41:56.626 align:middle
In all cases, you will get
forward inflation expectations

41:56.626 --> 41:58.236 align:middle
in the short term, I
think, that are going to be,

41:59.266 --> 42:01.136 align:middle
assuming that we're doing
our jobs appropriately,

42:01.136 --> 42:02.046 align:middle
that will be significant.

42:02.046 --> 42:04.906 align:middle
So you'll have a positive
[real] federal funds rate

42:06.396 --> 42:09.576 align:middle
at that point, which could be
1 percent or so, but-I mean,

42:09.576 --> 42:10.846 align:middle
I don't know exactly
what it would be.

42:10.846 --> 42:12.396 align:middle
But it would be significantly
positive

42:12.716 --> 42:13.526 align:middle
when we get to that level.

42:14.026 --> 42:15.986 align:middle
And let me say, we've written

42:15.986 --> 42:19.786 align:middle
down what we think
is a plausible path

42:19.856 --> 42:20.746 align:middle
for the federal funds rate.

42:21.356 --> 42:23.786 align:middle
The path that we actually
execute will be enough.

42:24.186 --> 42:26.156 align:middle
It will be enough to
restore price stability.

42:26.506 --> 42:29.546 align:middle
So this is something that, as
you can see, they've moved up,

42:30.096 --> 42:33.276 align:middle
and we're going to continue
to watch incoming data

42:33.276 --> 42:37.576 align:middle
and the evolving outlook and ask
ourselves whether our policy is

42:37.576 --> 42:38.686 align:middle
in the right place as we go.

42:39.676 --> 42:59.586 align:middle
Thank you very much.

